WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.320
Multilayer perceptron is a classic&nbsp;
feed-forward artificial neural network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.640
It is one of the core component of some deep&nbsp;
learning algorithms. Not all algorithms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.960
deep learning use a feed-forward artificial neural&nbsp;
network, but many do. Another way to look at this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.080
is that a multilayer perceptron is a connected&nbsp;
series of nodes, where each node represents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.920
a function. This connected series of node,&nbsp;
create what is called a directed acyclic graph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.360
Meaning that there is directionality between&nbsp;
the nodes and no node will ever be revisited.&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.040
&nbsp;
This all starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.760
with an input layer. Let's use the Titanic data&nbsp;
set example. There are four nodes here and each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.320
node would represent a feature that we want to&nbsp;
use to predict whether somebody survives or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.720
These could be age, ticket class, cabin and&nbsp;
sex. Next there's a hidden layer that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.800
representing with five nodes here. But this hidden&nbsp;
layer could contain 10 nodes, a hundred nodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:12.080
or even a thousand nodes. Now each of these nodes&nbsp;
represents some function. What happens is, we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.440
each of our input nodes, representing features and&nbsp;
we pipe those into each node in the hidden layer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.280
What's happening here is each node, or function,&nbsp;
in the hidden layer is getting each input feature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.800
So age, ticket class, cabin and sex are&nbsp;
passed into each node in the hidden layer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.120
You could view each of these nodes as&nbsp;
something like logistic regression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.720
So, more or less a logistic regression model&nbsp;
would be fit for each node made with slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.400
different weights.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.480
Then we have an output layer. And&nbsp;
here we're going to have two nodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.880
Either a person survived, or they did not survive,&nbsp;
using the example of our Titanic data set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.240
Now we have each node in the hidden layer,&nbsp;
outputting to each node in the output layer.&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.760
&nbsp;
Let's take a step back and just isolate one node&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.280
in the hidden layer to understand what's happening&nbsp;
here. You can see it's receiving the four inputs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.960
Then this one node in the hidden layer will&nbsp;
basically fit some model, or learn some aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.640
the data. Then based on that model that it learns,&nbsp;
it'll make some prediction about whether somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.600
survived or not. Maybe it's 70% likelihood that&nbsp;
they survived, 30% likelihood that they did not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.160
That's just one individual node in the hidden&nbsp;
layer. The same process can repeat for each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.720
the five nodes in the hidden layer, and you get&nbsp;
a final prediction of the likelihood of a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.560
person surviving.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:42.160
As you can imagine, these can be incredibly&nbsp;
powerful. As you see in this example, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.000
essentially aggregating the predictions&nbsp;
of five different models all together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.520
This allows the overall model, to learn some&nbsp;
really powerful relationships in the data.&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.920
When should you consider using&nbsp;
a multi-layer perceptron?&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.680
Up to this point we've suggested using&nbsp;
logistic regression and SVM only for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:08.000
a categorical or binary target variable.&nbsp;
One nice aspect of multilayer perceptrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.280
is that they're really powerful for both&nbsp;
classification and regression. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.600
they're useful for both categorical and continuous&nbsp;
target variables. As mentioned earlier, the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.720
multilayer perceptrons are constructed allow them&nbsp;
to learn very complex relationships in the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.480
If you have complex relationships in your data or&nbsp;
all you care about is performance and you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.280
care about training time or interpretability,&nbsp;
then this might be a good tool for your problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.400
There are a lot of hyperparameters to tune.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.160
If you want to have a lot of control over the&nbsp;
training process, multilayer perceptrons might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.640
be a good method to explore.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.000
When should you not use it? Multilayer&nbsp;
perceptrons are a core component of deep learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.520
You may have also heard that deep learning is&nbsp;
really good for tasks like image recognition or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:03.360
time series. That does not mean that multilayer&nbsp;
perceptrons are good for these kinds of problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.120
There are specific algorithms within deep&nbsp;
learning, like convolutional neural networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.880
that are very good for image recognition. So,&nbsp;
you should not use multilayer perceptrons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.640
this kind of work.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.000
If you care about transparency or significance&nbsp;
of predictors, you also shouldn't use multilayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.040
perceptrons. They're kind of like black boxes.&nbsp;
You pass in inputs and it returns predictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.200
and it's quite difficult to understand what's&nbsp;
going on internally that is generating those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.360
given predictions for any model. This is another&nbsp;
algorithm just like SVM that isn't great for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.520
quick benchmark model.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.400
Multilayer perceptrons require more&nbsp;
hyperparameter tuning than some other algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.000
so you need to spend time doing that&nbsp;
before you can expect to get a good model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.960
It can also take a while to train, though not&nbsp;
quite as long as SVM. So, this isn't a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.680
choice as a quick benchmark model.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.920
Lastly, you should not use multilayer perceptrons&nbsp;
if you have very limited data available. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.120
really thrive when you have a lot of data. If&nbsp;
you don't have a lot of data, you're probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.760
better off exploring other options as the time&nbsp;
investment likely won't pay off in performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.000
For instance, you might be better off with SVM or&nbsp;
logistic regression when you have limited data.&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.440
&nbsp;
In summary, multilayer perceptrons are often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.080
the best tool for the job when you have a lot of&nbsp;
data and you really only care about performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.000
But they're not ideal if you have limited data,&nbsp;
if you don't have much time or compute power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:47.600
or you really care about the transparency of&nbsp;
the model in understanding its predictions.

